# modules.cpp mm manifest specification

# Status and authority

This document specifies the mm manifest schema: every accepted format version,
every key each version introduces, the manifest kinds each key is valid on, the
order the shared gate applies those rules in, and the diagnostic each failure
produces. It describes implemented behaviour.

status: official gives this document the same authority as the other implemented
specifications in docs.

docs/mdy.mdy remains authoritative for the MDY file format itself: front matter,
metadata lines, repeated values, and body prose. This document governs what a
manifest may say, not how a file is written. docs/modules-configure.mdy remains
authoritative for what option, reset, and read-only mean once accepted;
docs/modules-platforms.mdy for the sdk and board keys; docs/modules-libraries.mdy
for the library keys and external-build; docs/modules.mdy for what each kind is
for. This document owns the version and key schema all of them share.

Examples use Opening fence and Closing fence instead of literal front matter
delimiters, following docs/mdy.mdy.

# One gate, every tool

The schema is enforced once, in mm.build's shared manifest gate, for configure,
build, test, check, model, document, run, and debug, including the standalone
test-manifest load. No front end implements its own copy of these rules, and a
tool rendering one manifest outside a walk reaches the same gate directly through
validate_manifest_schema.

The list of accepted versions in the diagnostic is generated from the same table
the rules are read from, so adding a version changes no message literal.

Configuration records are not manifests and do not pass through this gate. A record
under out declares kind: configuration with mm: 1.0 or mm: 2.0 and a schema key,
none of which this schema accepts: configuration is not one of the nine kinds, 2.0
is not an accepted version, and schema is not a key here. Records are generated
build-lane artifacts with their own private schema, specified by
docs/modules-platforms.mdy, and are read by a separate loader.

# Versions

Three versions are accepted.

Opening fence
1.0   unknown keys are kept
1.1   unknown keys are rejected
1.2   unknown keys are rejected
Closing fence

The mm key is required, appears exactly once, and must name an accepted version. A
manifest with no mm key, with two, or with an unrecognised value is rejected before
any other rule is applied.

A 1.0 manifest without newer declarations remains valid and inherits values and
locks normally from a newer parent. Its permissiveness is deliberate and is the
one behaviour that does not carry forward: unknown keys in a 1.0 manifest are not
diagnosed at all, while from 1.1 onward an unknown key is an error under a strict
traversal and a warning elsewhere.

1.3 and 1.4 are not accepted. Both existed during development, staging the library
and external-build keys, and both were folded into 1.2 before release. A manifest
declaring either is rejected as an unsupported version, which is asserted by test
rather than left to convention.

# Kinds

Nine kinds exist: project, dir, module, app, test, doc, sdk, board, and library. A
manifest declaring any other kind is rejected by name.

kind: library additionally requires mm: 1.2, checked against the kind itself. That
check is reached only when no key has already failed, so a 1.1 library manifest
declaring a 1.2 key reports the key and one declaring none reports the kind. Rule
order below states why. sdk and board carry no such floor: every key they need was
introduced at 1.2, so a 1.0 or 1.1 manifest of either kind fails on its first
platform key regardless.

# The keys

Every key, the version that introduced it, and the kinds it is valid on. A key is
valid only on the kinds listed.

Opening fence
1.0  mm                  project dir module app test doc sdk board library
1.0  kind                project dir module app test doc sdk board library
1.0  name                project dir module app test doc sdk board library
1.0  folder              project dir library
1.0  module              module
1.0  file                module app doc board
1.0  unit                test
1.0  use                 module app test

1.1  option              project dir module app test
1.1  reset               project dir module app test
1.1  read-only           project dir module app test

1.2  target              sdk
1.2  compiler-family     sdk
1.2  runtime             sdk
1.2  specs-profile       sdk
1.2  specs-file          sdk
1.2  sysroot             sdk
1.2  runtime-prefix      sdk
1.2  provides            sdk board
1.2  sdk                 board
1.2  cpu                 board
1.2  instruction-set     board
1.2  float-abi           board
1.2  security-domain     board
1.2  machine             board
1.2  linker-script       board
1.2  requires-board      app test
1.2  source              library
1.2  licence             library
1.2  include-directory   library
1.2  library-directory   library
1.2  link-archive        library
1.2  link-input          library
1.2  library             sdk module
1.2  external-build      library
1.2  platform-interface  module
1.2  platform-provider   sdk board
Closing fence

Three placements are worth stating because their absence is load-bearing rather
than accidental.

- file is not valid on sdk or library. An SDK declares a runtime and contributes
  no sources; a library describes a vendored tree, and rejecting file is what keeps
  foreign source out of the compiler and out of check's collection.
- option, reset, and read-only are not valid on doc, sdk, board, or library. Those
  kinds declare no code, so a local option value would have nothing to describe.
  They still inherit a resolved value from an ancestor; restricted declaration and
  inherited value are different things.
- library is valid on sdk and module and on no other kind. On an SDK it names the
  platform implementation; on a module it makes that module a wrapper.
- platform-interface is valid on module and platform-provider on sdk and board,
  and neither is valid on the other's kinds. The pair is one relationship read
  from two ends: a module declares that it is a portable interface, and a
  platform declares which module implements it. Neither key is valid on app or
  test, which is what keeps an application from naming its own provider.

# Rule order

The gate applies its rules in a fixed order, and the order decides which
diagnostic a manifest with several faults produces.

1. The document parsed, and mm names an accepted version, exactly once.
2. Every key, in sorted order: unknown keys, then the introducing version, then
   the valid kinds.
3. kind: library requires 1.2.
4. kind names one of the nine.
5. name is present and safe.

Two consequences follow, and both are observable rather than theoretical.

The whole key pass precedes every kind and name check, so a manifest that is wrong
in both ways reports the key. A 1.1 manifest declaring kind: library with a licence
key reports that licence requires 1.2; the same manifest with no 1.2 key reports
that kind library requires 1.2. Keys are visited in sorted order, not declaration
order, so the key named is the first failing one alphabetically: a manifest
declaring source, licence, and machine reports licence.

kind is itself a key with a kinds rule, listing the nine. An unrecognised kind
therefore fails that rule during the key pass and is reported as

Opening fence
<tool>: <manifest>: kind is not valid on kind: nonsense
Closing fence

rather than reaching the clearer unknown-kind diagnostic below, which survives for
the cases the key pass does not catch first.

Within one key, the introducing version is checked before the kinds, so a 1.0
manifest declaring target on a module reports the version requirement rather than
the placement. That is deliberate: raising the version is what makes the key legal
to write at all.

# Two value grammars this gate does not check

platform-interface is a marker: it occurs at most once and its value must be
empty. The module key on the same manifest already names the interface, so a
second spelling would only create a mismatch to diagnose. platform-provider is
repeated, and each value is exactly two module names separated by whitespace: the
interface, then its provider. A third field is rejected. Both grammars are the
owning specification's rules rather than this gate's, and are reported by the
code that reads them; they are stated here because the keys are otherwise
indistinguishable from the free-form ones above.

# Names

A name is required on every manifest. It may not be empty, may not be "." or "..",
and may not contain a path separator, because a name reaches a filesystem path and
a command line.

Names are unique within a kind, checked after the complete tree is collected. Two
SDKs, two boards, or two libraries sharing a name is an error naming both
manifests. Nodes of different kinds may share a name, which several parts of this
repository rely on: the pico directory and the pico board, or the pico-sdk library
and a module that wraps it.

# Diagnostics

Every failure names the manifest and the offending value.

Opening fence
<tool>: invalid or unsupported mm: version in <manifest> (supported: 1.0, 1.1, 1.2)
<tool>: <manifest>: kind library requires mm: 1.2
<tool>: unknown kind "<kind>" in <manifest>
<tool>: manifest has no name: <manifest>
<tool>: unsafe name "<name>" in <manifest>
<tool>: <manifest>: unknown manifest key: <key>
<tool>: <manifest>: unknown manifest key: <key> (ignored)
<tool>: <manifest>: <key> requires mm: <version>
<tool>: <manifest>: <key> is not valid on kind: <kind>
Closing fence

The two unknown-key forms differ by traversal, not by version: under the strict
folder-tree traversal configure requests, an unknown key in a 1.1 or 1.2 manifest
is fatal; under the default traversal every other tool uses, it is reported with
(ignored) and the walk continues. In a 1.0 manifest neither form appears.

A recognised declaration key whose value no lane tool consumes is reported
separately, and only by the tools that resolve options, as an ignored declaration
that does not stop the build.

Every schema failure is a manifest fault and exits 65.

# Compatibility

- A binary built before a version existed rejects a manifest declaring it as an
  unsupported version, naming the versions it supports. That is the intended
  diagnosis, and it is why a new key is introduced at a new version rather than
  added to a released one: an older binary must reject the manifest for the right
  reason rather than ignore the key or reject it as unknown.
- A newer binary reads every older version unchanged. Nothing introduced after 1.0
  changes the meaning of a 1.0 manifest.
- Raising a manifest's version is always safe on its own: no key changes meaning
  between versions, and the newer version only admits more keys and, from 1.1,
  stops ignoring unknown ones.

# Conformance

- A manifest with no mm key, with two, or with an unrecognised value is rejected,
  naming the supported versions.
- mm: 1.3 and mm: 1.4 are rejected as unsupported versions.
- A key declared before its introducing version is rejected naming that version;
  within one key that check precedes its kinds check.
- The key pass precedes the kind and name checks. A 1.1 library manifest with a
  1.2 key reports the key; the same manifest without one reports the kind. The key
  named is the first failing one in sorted order, not in declaration order.
- A key on a kind that does not accept it is rejected naming the kind, including
  file on an sdk manifest, target on a board manifest, requires-board on a module
  manifest, and option on a library manifest.
- kind: library in a 1.0 or 1.1 manifest declaring no 1.2 key is rejected naming
  the kind.
- A configuration record does not load through this gate, and a manifest declaring
  kind: configuration or mm: 2.0 is rejected.
- An unknown key is fatal under the strict traversal, reported and ignored under
  the default one, and invisible in a 1.0 manifest.
- An unknown kind, a missing name, and a name containing a path separator, ".", or
  ".." are each rejected.
- Two SDKs, two boards, or two libraries sharing a name are rejected naming both
  manifests; a library and a module may share one.
- platform-interface with a value, declared twice, or on any kind but module is
  rejected; so is platform-provider on any kind but sdk and board. In a 1.0 or
  1.1 manifest both are rejected as requiring 1.2 rather than accepted with an
  older meaning.
- A platform-provider value with one field or with three is rejected naming the
  declaration.
- Every manifest-reading tool applies these rules identically, including the
  standalone test-manifest load, and none requires a generated configuration
  record to do so.
- The supported-version diagnostic, the introduced-in diagnostic, and the
  unknown-key threshold all derive from one table; adding a version changes no
  message literal.

# Current boundaries

- There is no deprecation mechanism. A key is introduced at a version and valid
  from then on; nothing removes or renames one, and no manifest can declare which
  version it was written against beyond mm itself.
- The kinds list is closed in code rather than in data, so a new kind is a source
  change in the same place the key table lives.
- A key's value grammar is not part of this schema. The gate decides whether a key
  may appear; whether its value parses is the owning specification's rule and is
  reported by the code that reads it.
- Unknown keys in a 1.0 manifest remain undiagnosed. Tightening that would change
  the meaning of existing 1.0 manifests, which is the one compatibility promise
  this schema makes.
- An unrecognised kind is usually reported as kind is not valid on kind: X, because
  kind is checked as a key before the kind whitelist is reached. The clearer
  unknown-kind diagnostic exists and is mostly unreachable. Running the key pass
  after the kind and name checks would fix that, and would also make a 1.1 library
  manifest name its kind rather than one of its keys; it is a diagnostic
  improvement with no functional effect, and it changes which message an existing
  broken manifest produces.
